    {
      "type": "grammar",
      "id": "u17.d04.grammar",
      "title": {
        "en": "Explaining what you can see",
        "ru": "Объясняем видимое"
      },
      "rule": {
        "en": "We often use the present perfect continuous to explain a present situation or a visible result: someone is tired, wet or out of breath BECAUSE they have been doing something.",
        "ru": "Present perfect continuous часто объясняет нынешнее состояние или видимый результат: кто-то устал, промок или запыхался, ПОТОМУ ЧТО что-то делал."
      },
      "patterns": [
        {
          "label": {
            "en": "because … have been",
            "ru": "because … have been"
          },
          "formula": "… because + have been + -ing",
          "examples": [
            {
              "en": "I'm tired because I've been running.",
              "ru": "Я устал, потому что бегал."
            }
          ],
          "ref": "present-perfect-continuous"
        },
        {
          "label": {
            "en": "You look … Have you been…?",
            "ru": "Ты выглядишь… Ты…?"
          },
          "formula": "Have you been + -ing?",
          "examples": [
            {
              "en": "Your eyes are red. Have you been crying?",
              "ru": "У тебя красные глаза. Ты плакал?"
            }
          ],
          "ref": "present-perfect-continuous"
        },
        {
          "label": {
            "en": "visible result",
            "ru": "видимый результат"
          },
          "formula": "It's wet — it has been raining.",
          "examples": [
            {
              "en": "The ground is wet. It's been raining.",
              "ru": "Земля мокрая. Шёл дождь."
            }
          ],
          "ref": "present-perfect-continuous"
        }
      ],
      "pitfalls": [
        {
          "en": "The clue is a present result; the reason is the continuous: \"You're dirty — have you been gardening?\"",
          "ru": "Подсказка — нынешний результат; причина — длительная форма: «You're dirty — have you been gardening?»"
        },
        {
          "en": "Do not drop \"been\": \"Have you been crying?\", not \"Have you crying?\".",
          "ru": "Не теряй «been»: «Have you been crying?», а не «Have you crying?»."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "reading",
      "id": "u17.d04.reading",
      "title": {
        "en": "What happened to you?",
        "ru": "Что с тобой случилось?"
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "u17.d04.reading.b1",
          "en": "Tom came home late, wet and covered in mud. \"What have you been doing?\" asked his sister. \"You look terrible!\" Tom smiled. He had a good reason: he had been helping a neighbour find her lost dog in the rain.",
          "ru": "Том пришёл домой поздно, мокрый и в грязи. «Чем ты занимался? — спросила сестра. — Ты ужасно выглядишь!» Том улыбнулся. У него была веская причина: он под дождём помогал соседке искать потерявшуюся собаку.",
          "audio": {
            "src": "media/audio/u17.d04.reading.b1.mp3",
            "license": {
              "type": "original",
              "attribution": "Project authors (LLM-assisted)"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "u17.d04.reading.b2",
          "en": "\"We've been walking around the park for two hours,\" he said, \"but we found her in the end.\" He was exhausted and out of breath, but he was happy. Now he just wanted a hot shower and a chance to relax.",
          "ru": "«Мы два часа ходили по парку, — сказал он, — но в итоге нашли её». Он был измотан и запыхался, но был счастлив. Теперь он хотел лишь горячий душ и возможность отдохнуть.",
          "audio": {
            "src": "media/audio/u17.d04.reading.b2.mp3",
            "license": {
              "type": "original",
              "attribution": "Project authors (LLM-assisted)"
            }
          }
        }
      ],
